window.docContents[227].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-brooks-theorem-for-every-maximum-degree":{"contents":"We can now put the cases together. When Δ ≤ 1 there is nothing to prove: a graph\nwith no 1-clique has no vertices, and a graph with no 2-clique has no edges\n(cliqueFree_one and cliqueFree_two). So a graph of maximum degree at most Δ\nwith no (Δ + 1)-clique is Δ-colourable, unless Δ = 2 and one of its components\nis an odd cycle.\n\ntheorem brooks_general [G.LocallyFinite] {Δ : ℕ}\n    (hdeg : ∀ v, G.degree v ≤ Δ) (hcf : G.CliqueFree (Δ + 1))\n    (hcyc : Δ = 2 → ¬ ∃ (u : V) (c : G.Walk u u),\n      c.IsCycle ∧ Odd c.length ∧ ∀ v, G.Reachable u v → v ∈ c.support) :\n    G.Colorable Δ := by\n  rcases (by grind : Δ = 0 ∨ Δ = 1 ∨ Δ = 2 ∨ 3 ≤ Δ) with rfl | rfl | rfl | hΔ\n  · exact G.colorable_zero_iff.mpr (cliqueFree_one.mp hcf)\n  · obtain rfl := cliqueFree_two.mp hcf\n    exact ⟨Coloring.mk (fun _ => 0) fun h => h.elim⟩\n  · exact brooks_two hdeg (hcyc rfl)\n  · exact colorable_of_cliqueFree_of_degree_le hΔ hcf hdeg\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Brooks' Theorem*","header":"10.6.3. Brooks' Theorem for Every Maximum Degree","id":"/Connectivity/#section-brooks-theorem-for-every-maximum-degree"},"/Constructions/#section-cycle-graphs":{"contents":"For any n : ℕ we can define the cycle graph on n vertices as the graph with\nvertex set Fin n and edges between vertices that differ by 1 modulo n. Although\nwe usually think of cycles as always containing at least 3 vertices, in Mathlib we\nalso have cycle graphs on 0, 1, and 2 vertices. This is a common pattern in Mathlib:\nrather than add side conditions to the definition, we will instead need to check when\nusing cycleGraph whether or not it is in fact a cycle!\n\nFor 3 ≤ n the definition of adjacency is obvious:\nAdj a b := a - b = 1 ∨ b - a = 1. This also makes sense for n = 2 but gives us\nthe graph on Fin 2 with a single edge.\n\nThe cases n = 0 and n = 1 need a bit more care: for a b : Fin 0 we can't\nwrite a - b = 1 ∨ b - a = 1 since there is no 1 : Fin 0. While for a b : Fin 1\nwe can write a - b = 1 ∨ b - a = 1 but 1 = 0 in Fin 1 so this would define a\ngraph with loops, which is not allowed.\n\nexample : (1 : Fin 1) = 0 := rfl\n\n\nThe solution is to define cycleGraph n to be the empty graph for n = 0 and\nn = 1.\n\n#source cycleGraph --  def cycleGraph : (n : ℕ) → SimpleGraph (Fin n)\n                   --    | 0 | 1 => ⊥\n                   --    | _ + 2 => {\n                   --      Adj a b := a - b = 1 ∨ b - a = 1\n                   --    }\n\n\nThe cycle graphs C_3 and C_6\n\nWe introduce the notation C[n] for cycle graphs as follows:\n\n@[inherit_doc cycleGraph]\nscoped notation \"C[\" n \"]\" => cycleGraph n\n\n\n#check C[5]   -- C[5] : SimpleGraph (Fin 5)\nexample : C[5].Adj 0 1 := by decide\nexample : ¬ C[5].Adj 4 2 := by decide\n\n\nFor n = 0 and n = 1, C[n] is the empty graph on Fin n, while for n = 2 it\nis K[2]. The first two of these are definitional equalities (in the sense of\nDefinitional And Propositional Equalities), while the last is\nnot. (C[2].Adj a b is a - b = 1 ∨ b - a = 1 while K[2].Adj a b is a ≠ b. We\ncan prove that these are equivalent, but they are not definitionally equal.)\n\nexample : C[0] = E[0] := rfl\nexample : C[1] = E[1] := rfl\nexample : C[2] = K[2] := cycleGraph_two_eq_top\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Basic Families of Graphs","header":"3.1.4. Cycle Graphs","id":"/Constructions/#section-cycle-graphs"}});